title: Retries and flaky tests
section: Orchestration
desc: Retry scenarios, groups and whole runs, see which tests are flaky, and quarantine a known-bad test.
lede: A retry gives a failed test another chance. bddr still tells you it failed first, so retries do not hide flaky tests.
---
<h2>Retry a scenario</h2>
<div class="tabs" data-group="retry">
  <section data-tab="tag" data-label="Tag">
<cb lang="gherkin">@retry:2
Scenario: Load the dashboard</cb>
<p>Use <code>@noRetry</code> to turn retries off for a scenario when the config enables them.</p>
  </section>
  <section data-tab="config" data-label="Config">
<cb title="bddr.config.ts">retry: {
  scenario: 1,
  delay: '200ms',
  backoff: 2,
  on: ['ServiceUnavailable', '/timed out/'],
},</cb>
<p><code>on</code> limits retries to errors whose name or message matches. Without it every failure is retried.</p>
  </section>
  <section data-tab="cli" data-label="Command line">
<cb lang="bash">$ npx bddr run --retry 1</cb>
  </section>
</div>
<p>Each attempt starts with fresh scenario state and fresh scenario-scoped fixtures. The report shows every attempt.</p>

<h2>Flaky, not just passed</h2>
<p>A scenario that fails and then passes on a retry is reported as <span class="state flaky">flaky</span>. The run passes, unless you ask otherwise:</p>
<cb lang="bash">$ npx bddr run --retry 2 --fail-on-flaky</cb>
<p>With <code>history: true</code> in the config, bddr records every run in <code>.bddr/history</code> and gives each scenario a flakiness score: the share of runs whose outcome flipped or that passed only after a retry. A scenario that always fails scores 0, because it is broken, not flaky.</p>
<cb lang="bash">$ npx bddr history --top 10</cb>

<h2>Quarantine</h2>
<p>Add <code>@quarantine</code> to a test you know is broken but want to keep running. It runs and is reported, but a failure never fails the build. JUnit XML shows a quarantined failure as skipped. Scenarios that depend on a quarantined scenario are still skipped when it fails (see <a href="dependencies.html">Dependencies</a>).</p>
<cb lang="gherkin">@quarantine @owner:ann
Scenario: Export to PDF</cb>

<h2>Retry a group</h2>
<p>When members depend on each other, retrying one is not enough. Retry the group instead.</p>
<cb title="bddr.config.ts">groups: {
  warmup: { mode: 'serial', retry: 1, retryMembers: 'failed' },
},</cb>
<p><code>retryMembers</code> is <code>'all'</code> or <code>'failed'</code>. Groups that depend on this one wait until it settles.</p>

<h2>Retry a worker crash</h2>
<p>If a worker process crashes, only its scenario fails and the lane starts again. To retry the scenario on a fresh process, set <code>retry.workerCrash</code> or pass <code>--worker-crash-retry 1</code>.</p>

<h2>Re-run only what failed</h2>
<cb lang="bash">$ npx bddr run -f ndjson:reports/events.ndjson
$ npx bddr run --rerun-failed reports/events.ndjson -f ndjson:reports/events2.ndjson
$ npx bddr report reports/events.ndjson reports/events2.ndjson -f html:reports/merged.html</cb>
<p>The first run writes the event stream. The second runs only scenarios that did not pass, plus what they depend on. <code>bddr report</code> merges the files, and later runs win.</p>

<h2>Retry a step</h2>
<p>For one slow call, retry the step instead of the scenario. Put the tags at the end of the step line.</p>
<cb lang="gherkin">Then the report is ready @retry:5 @delay:2s @timeout:30s</cb>
<p>Defaults for every step of a scenario are <code>@stepRetry:N</code>, <code>@stepDelay:2s</code> and <code>@stepTimeout:10s</code>.</p>

<h2>Time budgets</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Budget</th><th>Set with</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>One step or hook</td><td><code>stepTimeoutMs</code> or <code>--timeout</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>One scenario</td><td><code>@timeout:2m</code> or <code>scenarioTimeout</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>One group</td><td><code>groups.name.timeout</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>The whole run</td><td><code>runTimeout</code> or <code>--run-timeout 30m</code></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd> once and bddr stops, cleans up, writes the reports and exits with code 130.</p>
